 think I think that is absolutely  true and one of the things that we've  been doing since Katrina is rebuilding  the city of New Orleans not the way that  she was but the way she always would  have been if we would have gotten it  right the first time and of course this  nation has had a really difficult time  struggling with the history of the Civil  War those monuments we'll put up  purposefully and intensely to send a  message that for some reason the  Confederacy was not going to become part  of the United States of America and it  was important to state really really  clearly that that's not who we were as a  people what's interesting is that some  people have said why does it all stop  taking down monuments Jon Meacham raised  that point in a piece that he wrote in  which he said the question and the  answer is was the person to whom a  monument is erected on public property  devoted to the American experiment in  Liberty and self-government Washington  and Jefferson and Andrew Jackson were  each owned slaves but each was committed  to the transparent idea yeah I think  he's correct about that there's a clear  delineation between revering certain  people and others these individuals put  these monuments up because to honor them  for trying to destroy the nation not to  support the nation to really deny the  issues of liberty and justice for  everybody not to support it and so  that's not who we ever were in the city  of New Orleans and so they didn't  reflect our values and so I thought it  was really important that we take them  down do you believe that those places  should be replaced by monuments to other  ideas and other people I think I think  the people in communities ought to use  their public's places in ways that lift  people up and not tear them down and I  think each community has to construct  either either monuments or other pieces  of art or green spaces that reflect who  they are as a people and that celebrate  who we are in an affirming way not at  not a destructive way those particular  monuments were put up by the Daughters  of the Confederacy pursuant to something  called the cult of the Lost Cause which  of course really honored us not being a  nation and and that's why it was really  important just to reflect what our true  history wasn't by the way they were put  up way after the Civil War that's what  kind of a historical lie right one of  the reasons that speech went so viral is  because you clearly put a lot of thought  into the words of the language that you  use I'm curious when you hear our  president speak and some of the colorful  language he chooses like yesterday even  at the United Nations calling kim  jungeun the rocket man what is your  reaction to some of the language this  president has used and and is it helpful  do you think I think I think it's  wrongheaded and I don't think it's  helpful at all this speech that I gave  was an invitation for people to look  into their souls and to really kind of  discern where we had been and where  we're gone and to come together as a  people to transform to a better place  that's what that was about it wasn't an  antagonistic speech designed to pummel  people or  beat them into submission that that  doesn't really work that speech  yesterday was actually sad and and and I  think that most Americans are going to  think that historically well like we  said the number of people who have been  tuning in and listening to your speech  continues to grow your words of wisdom  thank you so much for thank you very  much good to see you
 

